{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a02bd92c6a00d6f6", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# SpeechLLM-as-Judges: Towards General and Interpretable Speech Quality Evaluation\narXiv: 2510.14664\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Jinghua Zhao, Yifan Yang, Shujie Liu, Junyang Chen, Yanzhe Zhang, Shiwan Zhao, Jinyu Li, Jiaming Zhou, Haoqin Sun, Yan Lu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present SpeechEval, a large-scale dataset for speech quality evaluation, containing 128,754 annotations across languages, domains, and evaluation types.\n  2. We propose SQ-LLM, a speech-quality-aware model trained in two stages: instruction tuning with chain-of-thought reasoning and reward learning via GRPO.\n  3. We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of the SpeechLLM-as-Judges paradigm, demonstrating its advantages in accuracy, interpretability, and generalization across tasks and domains.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/LLM, LALM, LALM/后训练-GRPO", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d8ef6791-7e2e-4003-9523-7588caf24412", "uuid": "997d6859-3f1f-4e79-9c07-d356c19c252c", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T07:37:12.420Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# SpeechLLM-as-Judges: Towards General and Interpretable Speech Quality Evaluation\narXiv: 2510.14664\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Jinghua Zhao, Yifan Yang, Shujie Liu, Junyang Chen, Yanzhe Zhang, Shiwan Zhao, Jinyu Li, Jiaming Zhou, Haoqin Sun, Yan Lu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We present SpeechEval, a large-scale dataset for speech quality evaluation, containing 128,754 annotations across languages, domains, and evaluation types.\n  2. We propose SQ-LLM, a speech-quality-aware model trained in two stages: instruction tuning with chain-of-thought reasoning and reward learning via GRPO.\n  3. We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of the SpeechLLM-as-Judges paradigm, demonstrating its advantages in accuracy, interpretability, and generalization across tasks and domains.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/LLM, LALM, LALM/后训练-GRPO", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b8481bd82c4e0ba5", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# On The Landscape of Spoken Language Models: A Comprehensive Survey\narXiv: 2504.08528\nAuthors: Siddhant Arora, Kai-Wei Chang, Chung-Ming Chien, Yifan Peng, Haibin Wu, Yossi Adi, Emmanuel Dupoux, Hung-Yi Lee, Karen Livescu, Shinji Watanabe\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Provided a unified formulation and terminology for spoken language models (SLMs) by categorizing them into pure speech LMs, speech+text LMs, and speech-aware text LMs.\n  2. Categorized existing SLM research by detailing key design choices for model architecture, including speech encoders, modality adapters, sequence models, and speech decoders.\n  3. Outlined training strategies for SLMs by distinguishing between pre-training techniques and post-training methods like instruction tuning and preference optimization.\n  4. Reviewed architectural approaches for full-duplex spoken dialogue, specifically contrasting dual-channel and time-multiplexing strategies for handling simultaneous listening and speaking.\n  5. Organized existing evaluation methods and benchmarks for SLMs across likelihood-based metrics, generative metrics, interactivity measures, and trustworthiness assessments.\n  6. Identified key open challenges and future research directions, highlighting limitations in model architecture, training data, standardized evaluation, open-source availability, and safety.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/音频标记-融合, LALM/潜在表示", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d8ef6791-7e2e-4003-9523-7588caf24412", "uuid": "3927430a-6e1b-4566-b785-37a7f357e626", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T07:37:12.683Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# On The Landscape of Spoken Language Models: A Comprehensive Survey\narXiv: 2504.08528\nAuthors: Siddhant Arora, Kai-Wei Chang, Chung-Ming Chien, Yifan Peng, Haibin Wu, Yossi Adi, Emmanuel Dupoux, Hung-Yi Lee, Karen Livescu, Shinji Watanabe\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Provided a unified formulation and terminology for spoken language models (SLMs) by categorizing them into pure speech LMs, speech+text LMs, and speech-aware text LMs.\n  2. Categorized existing SLM research by detailing key design choices for model architecture, including speech encoders, modality adapters, sequence models, and speech decoders.\n  3. Outlined training strategies for SLMs by distinguishing between pre-training techniques and post-training methods like instruction tuning and preference optimization.\n  4. Reviewed architectural approaches for full-duplex spoken dialogue, specifically contrasting dual-channel and time-multiplexing strategies for handling simultaneous listening and speaking.\n  5. Organized existing evaluation methods and benchmarks for SLMs across likelihood-based metrics, generative metrics, interactivity measures, and trustworthiness assessments.\n  6. Identified key open challenges and future research directions, highlighting limitations in model architecture, training data, standardized evaluation, open-source availability, and safety.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/音频标记-融合, LALM/潜在表示", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
